---
import DocLayout from "../layouts/DocLayout.astro";
---

<DocLayout
  lang="en"
  page="privacy"
  section="privacy"
  eyebrow="Data and privacy"
  title="Your data starts in your workspace"
  intro="Opaline is designed as a local-first desktop knowledge workbench. Local editing does not require a cloud account."
  next={[{ href: "/docs/storage", label: "Storage architecture" }, { href: "/docs/ai", label: "Search, AI, and publishing" }]}
>
  <section>
    <h2>Where notes live</h2>
    <p>By default, Opaline creates a workspace under the user's documents folder. The durable content is ordinary files:</p>
    <pre><code>Documents/
  Opaline/
    notes/
    assets/
    .opaline/</code></pre>
    <ul>
      <li><code>notes/</code> stores durable HTML notes.</li>
      <li><code>assets/</code> stores images and attached files.</li>
      <li><code>.opaline/</code> stores app metadata, indexes, settings, cache, and history snapshots.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>HTML files are the source of truth. SQLite metadata should be rebuildable from notes whenever possible.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Network behavior</h2>
    <p>Opaline does not need a cloud account for local note editing. Network access can happen when a user explicitly configures or uses:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>AI providers, model tests, or model list fetching.</li>
      <li>Extension widgets or scripts that call network APIs.</li>
      <li>External links opened from notes.</li>
      <li>Manual update checks from Settings.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>AI keys and private notes</h2>
    <p>AI provider settings are user-configured, and API keys should be treated as secrets. Private notes should not be sent to cloud models without explicit user permission.</p>
    <p>Public alpha builds should not be used with sensitive production credentials unless the user has reviewed the code and understands the risk.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Extensions and backups</h2>
    <p>Extension widgets and live scripts are experimental. Only install extensions from sources you trust, and keep extension behavior explicit, visible, and reversible.</p>
    <p>Before testing alpha builds with important notes, back up the full workspace folder. A folder copy, a cloud-synced folder you control, or a private Git repository can all work depending on your notes.</p>
  </section>
</DocLayout>
